.El
.It Fl P Ar port
Specifies the port to connect to on the remote host.
Note that this option is written with a capital
.Sq P ,
because
.Fl p
is already reserved for preserving the times and mode bits of the file.
.It Fl p
Preserves modification times, access times, and file mode bits from the
source file.
.It Fl q
Quiet mode: disables the progress meter as well as warning and diagnostic
messages from
.Xr hpnssh 1 .
.It Fl R
Copies between two remote hosts are transferred through the local host
by default; such through-local transfers do not support
.Fl j ,
.Fl V ,
or
.Fl Z ,
and refuse to run rather than silently ignore them.
This option instead copies between two remote hosts by connecting to the origin
host and executing
.Nm
there, and supports all three.
This requires that
.Nm
running on the origin host can authenticate to the destination host without
requiring a password.
.Pp
A non-default destination port may be given by writing the destination
as an
.Ar scp://
URI; it is forwarded to the origin host's
.Nm
along with any
.Fl j
parallel-streams request.
When run from a terminal, a progress meter for the remote transfer is
displayed when the origin host runs a current HPN-SSH
.Nm ;
against older or stock OpenSSH origin hosts the transfer proceeds
without a meter.
.It Fl r
Recursively copy entire directories.
Note that
.Nm
follows symbolic links encountered in the tree traversal.
.It Fl Z
Resume a failed or interrupted transfer with integrity verification.
The local and remote copies are compared by hashing them in ranges; only
the ranges that differ are re-transferred, and the repaired file is then
re-verified.
A file whose contents already match is skipped.
This relies on the SFTP protocol's random-access range hashing and so
cannot be combined with the legacy SCP protocol
.Pq Fl O .
This is an
.Nm
only option.
.It Fl S Ar program
Name of
.Ar program
to use for the encrypted connection.
The program must understand
.Xr hpnssh 1
options.
.It Fl s
Use the SFTP protocol for transfers rather than the original scp protocol.
.It Fl T
Disable strict filename checking.
By default when copying files from a remote host to a local directory
.Nm
checks that the received filenames match those requested on the command-line
to prevent the remote end from sending unexpected or unwanted files.
Because of differences in how various operating systems and shells interpret
filename wildcards, these checks may cause wanted files to be rejected.
This option disables these checks at the expense of fully trusting that
the server will not send unexpected filenames.
.It Fl V
Verify the integrity of every transferred file
.Pq HPNSSH extension .
After each file is transferred its local and remote copies are hashed with
XXH3 and compared via the
.Cm hpn-check-file@hpnssh.org
extension; a mismatch is logged and reported and
.Nm
exits with code 57
.Pq Dv SFTP_EX_VERIFY_FAILED ,
though the transfer itself is not aborted.
Note the capital letter:
.Fl v
is verbose.
.It Fl v
Verbose mode.
Causes
.Nm
and
.Xr hpnssh 1
to print debugging messages about their progress.
This is helpful in
debugging connection, authentication, and configuration problems.
.It Fl w Ar writers_per_file
Set the maximum number of parallel workers that may write byte ranges into
the same file at the same time.
When a large file is split into byte ranges
.Pq see Fl M ,
several workers would otherwise write to it concurrently; because writes into a
single file serialise on a per-file lock, splitting a file across too many
workers can be slower than using fewer.
This option bounds that concurrency per file; the effective limit is always the
smaller of this value and
.Fl j .
Must be in the range
.Bq 1, 10 .
The default is 4.
Lowering it reduces per-file write contention and run-to-run variance at the
cost of single-file throughput; raising it allows more concurrent writers per
file, which can re-introduce the contention this option exists to avoid.
In a multi-file transfer, workers not permitted to write a file that is at its
cap are not idle: they pick up byte ranges of other files.
This flag only takes effect together with
.Fl j .
Without
.Fl j
the parallel-streams orchestrator is not engaged and the option is a no-op.
.It Fl X Ar sftp_option
Specify an option that controls aspects of SFTP transfer behaviour.
The valid options, whose names and values are case-insensitive, are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm nrequests Ns = Ns Ar value
Controls how many concurrent SFTP read or write requests may be in progress
at any point in time during a download or upload.
This value must be between 1 and 8192.
By default 1024 requests may be active concurrently.
.It Cm buffer Ns = Ns Ar value
Controls the maximum buffer size for a single SFTP read/write operation used
during download or upload. This value must be between 1B and 255KB. You may use
the K unit for the size. E.g. 32768 or 32K.
By default a 32KB buffer is used.
.It Cm VerifyRepair Ns = Ns Cm yes Ns | Ns Cm no
When transfer verification is enabled, controls whether a verification
failure is automatically repaired by re-transferring and re-verifying only
the affected byte ranges, bounded by a per-range retry limit, rather than
failing the transfer.
The default is
.Cm yes .
Setting
.Cm VerifyRepair Ns = Ns Cm no
disables auto-repair, so a verification failure exits non-zero as before.
.El
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std scp
.Pp
As an HPNSSH extension, when integrity verification is requested
.Pq Fl V
and one or more files fail the post-transfer XXH3 integrity
check, the transfer is completed but
.Nm
exits with code 57
.Pq Dv SFTP_EX_VERIFY_FAILED
and prints a summary of the files that did not match their source. This
distinct code lets automation detect a silent-corruption event separately
from an ordinary transfer error.
